      "question_text": "4 Consider the following conjecture: If N is a positive integer that consists of the digit 1 followed by an odd number of 0 digits and then a ﬁnal digit 1, then N is a prime number. Here are three numbers: I N = 101 (which is a prime number) II N = 1001 (which equals 7 × 11 × 13) III N = 10001 (which equals 73 × 137) Which of these provide(s) a counterexample to the conjecture?",
